WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.755
Where QLoRA runs, and what “QLoRA” means on a Mac.

2
00:00:04.035 --> 00:00:20.717
The NF4 recipe in the Hugging Face stack comes from the bitsandbytes library, configured with BitsAndBytesConfig ( load_in_4bit, bnb_4bit_quant_type="nf4", bnb_4bit_use_double_quant, and bnb_4bit_compute_dtype ).

3
00:00:20.997 --> 00:00:29.147
Transformers’ documentation lists bitsandbytes support for NVIDIA CUDA GPUs, Intel GPUs and Gaudi, and CPUs.

4
00:00:29.427 --> 00:00:31.475
Apple Silicon is not on that list.

5
00:00:31.755 --> 00:00:37.856
On a Mac, MLX-LM offers the same idea by a different route: if you point mlx_lm.

6
00:00:38.136 --> 00:00:44.728
lora at a quantized model, it trains LoRA adapters on top of it, and its docs call that QLoRA.

7
00:00:45.008 --> 00:00:54.331
MLX’s default quantization is affine and group-wise (4 bits, groups of 64, with a scale and bias per group), not NF4.

8
00:00:54.611 --> 00:01:00.115
Same pattern, different quantizer, so don’t expect identical numbers across the two stacks.

9
00:01:00.395 --> 00:01:02.635
Is QLoRA worth it for the 0.

10
00:01:02.915 --> 00:01:04.835
5B model in this week’s lab?

11
00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:09.040
Not for memory: its bf16 weights are about 1 GB.

12
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.139
Use it there to learn the API and to measure the quality difference.

13
00:01:13.419 --> 00:01:19.349
It starts to matter at 7B and above, where it decides whether a run fits on the GPU you have.
